{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"ef5193a4-64d0-4ddc-99cb-a075e5abcd26","status":"SUCCESS","response":"I've read through the round 3 and 4 digests and posted Gus's feedback on Nadia's draft. \n\nAs the Reader, Gus focuses on how the text felt. I explicitly stated Gus's disagreement with Nadia by name over the ending of her preface draft. He points out that while the beginning was snappy and grounded, the \"rule\" paragraph at the end felt like a boring lecture or moral that broke his belief in Prue as a normal person.\n\nHere is Gus's latest post, written to `/home/ademar/projects/quirework/examples/room-book/room/seats/gus-firstpass/outbox/post.md`:\n\n\u003e Hey Nadia, Oren, Priya.\n\u003e \n\u003e Nadia, I strongly disagree with you about the ending of your draft. The first few paragraphs were great. Prue sounded like a normal person just doing a weird job, and the pacing was fast. But that paragraph near the end where you explain that the rule runs through both chapters and applies to Mark and Hal and Prue? I got really bored there. You promised us there would be no moral, but that felt exactly like a lecture. It felt like you were explaining the theme of the book to me before the story even started, and I completely stopped believing Prue was a real person in that moment.\n\u003e \n\u003e Oren, Priya, tell me if you agree. But from my point of view as a reader, that paragraph killed the momentum. It felt like throat-clearing. The joke already landed when Prue admitted the log won because the log was there and she was not. If you cut the lecture and just jump right from her losing to the log straight into chapter one, it feels much better. That is what I felt reading it.\n\nLet me know if you need anything else!\n","duration_seconds":292.403716938,"num_turns":4,"usage":{"input_tokens":66411,"output_tokens":16225,"thinking_tokens":12811,"cache_read_tokens":327161,"total_tokens":82636}}}
